---
import Sheet from "../layouts/Sheet.astro";
import { REVS } from "../data/revs.js";
import { PRESS, SENRA, CAREERS, EMAIL, ROLES } from "../data/site.js";
const newestFirst = [...REVS].reverse();
---

<Sheet
  description="Co-founder of Senra, where we build wiring for aerospace and defense companies. I apply frontier AI and robotics to automate high-mix assembly at scale."
  sheet="1"
  dwg="BS-001"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero__text reveal is-in">
      <h1 class="sr-only">Benjamin Shanahan</h1>
      <p class="intro">
        Hi, I'm Ben. I co-founded <a
          href={SENRA}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer">Senra</a
        >, where we build wiring for aerospace and defense companies.
      </p>
      <p class="lede">
        These days I'm applying frontier AI and robotics to automate high-mix
        assembly at scale. Before Senra I wrote manufacturing software at
        SpaceX, and before that I studied neuroscience at Brown and worked on
        brain-computer interfaces.
      </p>
    </div>

    <aside class="hire reveal is-in" aria-label="Hiring">
      <p class="hire__tag mono">
        <span class="sq" aria-hidden="true"></span>ECN 0001 &nbsp; Open roles
      </p>
      <p>
        I'm hiring 0-to-1 engineers to build Senra's robotics program from the
        ground up with me. You'll build and iterate right alongside the
        technicians who use what you make. The roles are in Cypress, CA, and
        report directly to me.
      </p>
      {
        ROLES.length > 0 && (
          <ul class="hire__roles">
            {ROLES.map(([title, href]) => (
              <li>
                <a class="ext" href={href} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
                  {title}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        )
      }
      <div class="actions mono">
        <a
          class="btn btn--hl ext"
          href={CAREERS}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer">Come build with me</a
        >
        <a class="btn ext" href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>Email me</a>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <!-- revision list on the left, Fig. 1 on the right; the list scrolls within the figure's height -->
    <div class="trace">
      <section class="trace__list" aria-labelledby="revs">
        <h2 id="revs" class="sr-only">Revision history</h2>
        <div class="trace__scroll">
          <table class="table">
            <thead><tr><th>Rev</th><th>Revisions</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                newestFirst.map((r) => (
                  <tr data-rev={r.rev}>
                    <td class="rev">
                      <button
                        class="rev-toggle"
                        type="button"
                        aria-label={`Highlight revision ${r.rev}: ${r.title}`}
                        aria-pressed="false"
                        disabled
                      >
                        {r.rev}
                      </button>
                    </td>
                    <td>
                      <span class="t-date mono">{r.date}</span>
                      <span class="t-title">
                        {r.title}{" "}
                        {r.tag && (
                          <span class={r.now ? "tag tag--hl" : "tag"}>
                            {r.tag}
                          </span>
                        )}
                      </span>
                      <Fragment set:html={r.desc} />
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <figure class="figure">
        <div id="brain" data-revs={JSON.stringify(REVS)}></div>
        <figcaption class="mono">
          <span>Fig. 1 &nbsp; Human-machine interface assy</span>
          <span>Not to scale</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block reveal" aria-labelledby="about">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="about">About</h2><span class="mono">Notes</span>
    </div>
    <div class="about">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I built my first website in 2005, when I was ten. A few years later I
          got a LEGO Mindstorms kit for my birthday, and it started an obsession
          with building things that interact with the world. In high school my
          dad gave me an Arduino starter kit over the holidays, and I taught
          myself to solder, read schematics, and build simple circuits and
          robots. To me, building hardware and software is as creative as art or
          music, and some of my favorite projects mix them, like <a
            href="/projects/audiolux/">Audiolux</a
          >, a light show that reacts to music in real time.
        </p>
        <p>
          At Brown I studied neuroscience and found my way into brain-computer
          interface research in the BrainGate lab, where hardware, software and
          my degree all came together. I wrote my honors thesis there on
          detecting idle-state brain activity with machine learning classifiers,
          and graduated magna cum laude in 2017. I stayed on for a year after
          that. I built a real-time monitoring system for the research cart, and
          worked with a grad student to miniaturize the cart, shrinking its
          amplifiers and signal processing onto a single FPGA, which taught me
          to think in clock cycles. I also reverse-engineered the lab's
          recording software so our version could drop in for the cart, writing
          the same data format all of BrainGate's analysis was built on.
        </p>
        <p>
          Next I moved to Silicon Valley to join NeuroPace, which makes an
          implanted device for epilepsy. Embedded with the research team, I
          built web-based visualization and treatment-suggestion tools that
          brought their machine learning research to doctors. Looking back,
          BrainGate and NeuroPace gave me an early start on the building blocks
          of today's AI: classifiers, neural nets, LSTMs, and t-SNE for making
          sense of high-dimensional neural data. Mid-pandemic I moved to LA to
          join SpaceX, where I worked on Warpdrive, on the manufacturing
          execution system (MES) that built Starlink user terminals and Wi-Fi
          routers at high rate, and briefly on telemetry systems that record
          continuous high-rate data from the whole Starlink fleet.
        </p>
        <p>
          At SpaceX I met Jordan Black, and in 2023 we started <a
            href={SENRA}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer">Senra</a
          > to fix the wire harness design, procurement, and quality problems SpaceX
          and countless other customers kept running into. We'd both built and used
          high- and low-rate manufacturing systems firsthand, and we knew the solution
          consisted of an end-to-end software platform and a smart factory built in
          tandem from the ground up. For the first three years I built our harness
          design tool and ERP/MES with the software team, which we later merged into
          one platform, Amp. Since mid-2026 I've been leading our frontier automation
          program, using robotics and AI to speed up the build for our technicians.
        </p>
        <p>
          What drives me is understanding how things work and fit together. I
          love hard problems with no clear solution, and I like to stick with
          them until the pieces click. I've always been a 0-to-1 guy, and Senra
          is the best version of that I've found: a problem that matters to the
          whole industrial base, where the answer spans process, people, and a
          lot of technology stitched together at scale. Most of my free time
          goes into Senra too. Beyond that I make electronic music and art,
          climb, play volleyball, run, hike, bike, cook when I have time, go on
          walks with my wife, and pick up the occasional <a href="/projects/"
            >hobby project</a
          >.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="portrait">
        <div class="portrait__img">
          <img
            src="/img/ben-portrait.jpg"
            alt="Benjamin Shanahan"
            width="866"
            height="1294"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <i class="crop crop--tl"></i><i class="crop crop--tr"></i><i
            class="crop crop--bl"></i><i class="crop crop--br"></i>
        </div>
        <figcaption class="mono">
          <span>Fig. 2</span><span>B. Shanahan</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block reveal" aria-labelledby="press">
    <div class="head"><h2 id="press">Reference documents</h2></div>
    <table class="table">
      <thead><tr><th>Ref</th><th>Article</th><th class="hide-sm">Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          PRESS.map(([title, href, source], i) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="rev">R{i + 1}</td>
              <td><a class="t-title ext" href={href} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">{title}</a></td>
              <td class="date hide-sm">{source}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
</Sheet>

<script>
  import { buildBrain } from "../scripts/brain.js";
  const host = document.getElementById("brain");
  if (host) buildBrain(host, JSON.parse(host.dataset.revs));
</script>
